            "The Berth Allocation Problem (BAP) entails assigning a set of arriving container vessels to specific berthing locations along the terminal wharf, and determining the exact arrival, mooring, and departure timestamps for each ship over a rolling planning horizon. Mathematically, BAP formulations are classified along two dimensions: spatial configuration and temporal dynamics. Spatially, the berth can be modeled as Discrete (the wharf is partitioned into fixed, numbered berthing slots where only one ship can dock at a time) or Continuous (the wharf is treated as a continuous physical line, allowing vessels of varying lengths to moor at any coordinate, maximizing spatial packing efficiency). Temporally, BAP can be Static (all vessels are assumed to have already arrived and wait in the harbor anchorage) or Dynamic (vessels have specific expected times of arrival, ETA, and cannot moor before arriving).",
            "In its standard continuous dynamic formulation, the Berth Allocation Problem can be visualized geometrically as a Two-Dimensional Non-Overlapping Rectangle Packing Problem. The horizontal axis represents Time (hours over the weekly horizon), and the vertical axis represents Space (continuous meter distance along the physical quay). Each arriving vessel $i$ is represented as a two-dimensional rectangle: its width corresponds to the vessel's Total Service Time ($S_i$, the duration required to load and discharge its containers), and its height corresponds to the vessel's Overall Length ($L_i$), augmented by mandatory safety clearance buffers between adjacent ships. The mathematical objective function universally minimizes total vessel port turnaround time and tardiness penalties: $\\max \\text{or} \\min \\sum_{i=1}^N \\left( c_i^{\\text{wait}} (B_i - A_i) + c_i^{\\text{serv}} S_i + c_i^{\\text{late}} \\max(0, C_i - D_i) \\right)$, where $A_i$ is expected arrival time, $B_i$ is actual berthing time, $C_i$ is departure completion time, $D_i$ is contracted departure deadline, and $c_i$ represents weighted priority coefficients.",
